      values: [
        VizFieldFull {
          label: 'Orders/User'
          ref: r(ecom_cohort_sql_analysis.sql_orders_per_user)
          format {
            type: 'number'
            pattern: '#,###0.00'
          }
          uname: 'opuw_val'
        }
      ]
      settings {
        conditional_formats: [
          ConditionalFormat {
            key: 'opuw_val'
            format: ScaleFormat {
              min {
                value: 0
                color: '#FFFFFF'
              }
              mid {
                value: 1.5
                color: '#D1E5FA'
              }
              max {
                value: 3
                color: '#8DBEF2'
              }
            }
          }
        ]
        sorts: [
          SortSetting {
            key: 'opuw_row'
            direction: 'asc'
          },
          SortSetting {
            key: 'opuw_col'
            direction: 'asc'
          }
        ]
        frozen_columns: 1
        wrap_header_text: false
        pagination_size: 13
      }
    }
  }

  block txt_opu_why: TextBlock {
    content: @md **What the difference does.** The repeat-purchase curve flattens, because only orders containing the chosen category are counted — a customer ordering monthly in other categories looks like a one-time buyer.

**The second report describes a different population.** Filtering at the final-results level selects everyone who *ever* bought the category rather than those who bought it at acquisition, so Cohort Size itself moves: for Jan 2026 / Skin Care it reads 210 against 83. Month 0 is where it shows plainest — every acquired customer orders in their acquisition month by definition, so retention there is 100% when the filter profiles the cohort, and 44.8% when it only trims the output.;;
  }

