  block txt_sum_why: TextBlock {
    content: @md **What the difference does.** Every column shifts at once here, and Cohort Size shifts with them — which is the clearest sign that the second filter is selecting a different population rather than trimming a report.

**The second report describes a different population.** Filtering at the final-results level selects everyone who *ever* bought the category rather than those who bought it at acquisition, so Cohort Size itself moves: for Jan 2026 / Skin Care it reads 210 against 83. Month 0 is where it shows plainest — every acquired customer orders in their acquisition month by definition, so retention there is 100% when the filter profiles the cohort, and 44.8% when it only trims the output.;;
  }
  block txt_ov_hero: TextBlock {
    content: @md ## Cohort reports and the grain problem

Every cohort question mixes two things that live in different places: who the customer is, and what they did afterwards.

That sounds trivial. It is the reason cohort reports are awkward to build and easy to get quietly wrong.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_models: TextBlock {
    content: @md <div style="display:flex;align-items:stretch;gap:0">
  <div style="flex:1;background:#E8F2FD;border:1px solid #8DBEF2;border-radius:6px;padding:12px 14px">
    <div style="font-weight:700;color:#05264C;font-size:15px">Users</div>
    <div style="font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1B7CE4;margin:3px 0 8px">one row per customer</div>
    <div style="font-size:13px;color:#212327;line-height:1.5">Who they are, and when they first bought.</div>
  </div>
  <div style="width:34px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:#CBD0D7;font-size:20px">›</div>
  <div style="flex:1;background:#F9FBFC;border:1px solid #E3E7ED;border-radius:6px;padding:12px 14px">
    <div style="font-weight:700;color:#05264C;font-size:15px">Orders</div>
    <div style="font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#989CA6;margin:3px 0 8px">one row per order</div>
    <div style="font-size:13px;color:#212327;line-height:1.5">When they came back.</div>
  </div>
  <div style="width:34px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:#CBD0D7;font-size:20px">›</div>
  <div style="flex:1;background:#E8F2FD;border:1px solid #8DBEF2;border-radius:6px;padding:12px 14px">
    <div style="font-weight:700;color:#05264C;font-size:15px">Order Items</div>
    <div style="font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1B7CE4;margin:3px 0 8px">one row per line</div>
    <div style="font-size:13px;color:#212327;line-height:1.5">What was in each order, and what it cost.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;border-top:2px dashed #8DBEF2;margin-top:10px;padding-top:8px;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#1663B6">
  <span>which customers did skincare win us …</span>
  <span>… and what were they worth</span>
</div>

<p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:14px;color:#505157;line-height:1.6">One real question, and it lands on the two ends. Nothing in the middle knows about either, and the answer needs both at once.</p>;;
  }

  block txt_ov_sql: TextBlock {
    content: @md ### Why this is hard in SQL

<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin:8px 0 14px">
  <div style="display:flex;gap:3px">
    <div style="width:26px;height:34px;background:#E3E7ED;border-radius:3px"></div>
    <div style="width:26px;height:34px;background:#E3E7ED;border-radius:3px"></div>
    <div style="width:26px;height:34px;background:#E3E7ED;border-radius:3px"></div>
  </div>
  <div style="color:#989CA6;font-size:18px">›</div>
  <div style="flex:1;background:#FFF7EA;border:1px solid #FECF7F;border-radius:6px;padding:10px 14px;font-size:13px;color:#212327">
    <strong style="color:#B04A00">one pre-joined table</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; the cohort is now a column in it, alongside category, country and the rest
  </div>
</div>

The numbers are right. But filtering skincare stops meaning *customers acquired by skincare* and starts meaning *rows where the category is skincare*, so everything else those customers bought drops out of the answer. The report still renders. It just quietly answers a different question.

You can reach for query parameters. Then you maintain a parameter, a default value, and a matching branch inside every metric, for each attribute anyone might one day want to filter on. Granularity is worse: months since acquisition was computed and frozen, so showing weeks means a second model.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_aql: TextBlock {
    content: @md ### What AQL does instead

<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin:8px 0 14px">
  <div style="background:#E8F2FD;border:1px solid #8DBEF2;border-radius:6px;padding:8px 12px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#05264C">Users</div>
  <div style="flex:1;border-top:2px solid #1B7CE4;position:relative;text-align:center">
    <span style="background:#FFFFFF;padding:0 10px;font-size:12px;color:#1663B6;font-weight:600;position:relative;top:-9px">dimensionalize() lifts the fact up to the person</span>
  </div>
  <div style="background:#E8F2FD;border:1px solid #8DBEF2;border-radius:6px;padding:8px 12px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#05264C">Order Items</div>
</div>

Cohort membership becomes a property of a person rather than a column in a result set. Filter it and you are selecting people; the metrics carry on measuring everything those people did.

Granularity works the same way. `Cohort Period` and `Period Number` both read one control, so switching it re-cuts the rows and the columns together. Nothing was written in advance for weeks, or quarters, or for skincare.;;
  }

  block txt_ov_cost: TextBlock {
    content: @md ### What it costs, in practice

<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:8px 0 14px">
  <tr>
    <th style="text-align:left;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:2px solid #E3E7ED;color:#666970;font-weight:600">Ask it to…</th>
    <th style="text-align:left;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:2px solid #1B7CE4;background:#F5F8FA;color:#05264C;width:26%">Semantic layer</th>
    <th style="text-align:left;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:2px solid #E36A01;background:#FFF7EA;color:#05264C;width:26%">SQL model</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #EDF1F5;color:#212327">Profile a cohort by what they bought at acquisition</td>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #EDF1F5;background:#F5F8FA;color:#1F815A;font-weight:600">Picks the customers</td>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #EDF1F5;background:#FFF7EA;color:#B04A00;font-weight:600">Cuts the rows</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #EDF1F5;color:#212327">Switch to weekly or quarterly cohorts</td>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #EDF1F5;background:#F5F8FA;color:#1F815A;font-weight:600">One control</td>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #EDF1F5;background:#FFF7EA;color:#B04A00;font-weight:600">A second model</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;color:#212327">Slice by an attribute nobody asked for last quarter</td>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;background:#F5F8FA;color:#1F815A;font-weight:600">Add one field</td>
    <td style="padding:9px 10px;background:#FFF7EA;color:#B04A00;font-weight:600">Rewrite and re-test</td>
  </tr>
</table>

With no filters set, both builds on every tab return the same numbers. That is worth sitting with for a second: the SQL version is not sloppy, it is finished. It answers what it was built to answer and cannot be talked into answering anything else.

<div style="background:#05264C;border-radius:6px;padding:12px 16px;color:#FFFFFF;font-size:14px;margin-top:12px">
Open any tab, set <strong>Category</strong> to <code style="background:#104A89;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:3px;color:#FFFFFF">Skin Care</code>, then move <strong>Cohort Granularity</strong> to <code style="background:#104A89;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:3px;color:#FFFFFF">Week</code>.
</div>;;
  }

